  "text": "elcome, user! To solve the problem, we need to understand what the \"closed curve\" traced by the incenter of triangle ABC looks like when point C is moving around the circle. The incenter of a triangle is the point of intersection of the three angle bisectors. In this case, as point C moves around the circle, the incenter traces out a path that is essentially the circle itself, except for two points where the incenter falls outside the triangle when the triangle is degenerate.\n\nThis is because as C moves along the circle, the distances from A, B, and C to the incenter (the point of intersection of the angle bisectors) will trace out the same circle, just scaled down. The two points where the incenter misses are exactly where the circumcircle touches the circle, which are the points diametrically opposite AB.\n\nThe perimeter of the region bounded by this curve is actually the length of one of the circles, which has the same radius as the original circle, because the incenter's path is essentially the perimeter of the original circle itself minus the two points where it misses. Since the original circle has diameter AB = 18, its radius is 9. The length of the circle is 2 * π * radius = 2 * π * 9 = 18π. \n\nTo the nearest positive integer, 18π ≈ 56.55, which rounds up to 57. \n\nTherefore, the final answer is:\n\n\\boxed{57}",
